{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 54, :comments ["<b>To the large you shall increase his inheritance</b> – To a tribe numerous in population they gave a large portion, and even though the portions were not equal — for they divided the portions according to the multitude of the tribe — they nevertheless did it only by means of a lot, and the lot was by means of the Holy Spirit; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 26:54).\n\nBut the midrash of our Sages on this verse is not so. Rather, they said in the Sifrei (Pinchas 26:3): \"To the large you shall increase his inheritance\" — this teaches that if there went out with him ten sons from Egypt, and upon their entry into the land five were found, etc. And so too in the Gemara it is explicit that its meaning among the Sages is not to divide between tribe and tribe at all, as they said (Bava Batra 117a): \"According to the one who says to those who went out from Egypt, that is what is written 'to the large you shall increase his inheritance,' etc.\" And furthermore, I have already written in the portion of Vayehi Yaakov (Genesis 48:6) that in the Gemara in the chapter Yesh Nochalin (Bava Batra 122a) they raised in explanation that the land was not divided according to the skull-count of the men, but rather it was divided among the tribes: they made twelve equal portions from it, and each tribe took the portion that came out for it by lot. And this is what the children of Joseph cried out, saying \"Why have you given me a possession of one lot and one portion, and I am a numerous people\" (Joshua 17:14). And Joshua did not add anything to them, but said to him \"Go up to the forest country and clear for yourself there in the land of the Perizzite and the Rephaim, since the hill country of Ephraim is too narrow for you\" (v. 15), meaning that they should conquer for themselves from the land that had not been conquered for them, and thereby expand their territory.\n\nAnd this is what is said: \"By the names of the tribes of their fathers they shall inherit… between the many and the few\" (Numbers 26:55–56), that all the tribes shall take equally, whether the tribe is numerous in population or few in population. And the meaning of \"to the large you shall increase his inheritance\" would then apply to the men of the tribe itself. Or, according to its midrash that it was divided among those who went out from Egypt — if there went out with him ten from Egypt, and upon their entry into the land five were found, I call concerning it \"to the large you shall increase,\" meaning that if those ten who were twenty years old at their departure from Egypt died, and five sons were born to them in the wilderness, or they were minors and became twenty years old, I call concerning it \"to the large you shall increase,\" meaning: to the one who was large at the exodus from Egypt you shall increase, even though he is now few. If there went out with him five and upon their entry into the land ten were found, I call concerning it \"to the few you shall diminish,\" as is stated in the Sifrei.\n\nBut I saw in the Sifrei there that they taught: \"According to their counted ones\" — this teaches that the land was not divided except to each and every tribe according to what it is, as it says \"And the children of Joseph spoke to Joshua, saying, 'Why have you given me a possession of one lot and one portion, and I am a numerous people, for so far HaShem has blessed me.'\" What does he say? \"And Joshua said to them, 'If you are a numerous people, go up to the forest country and clear for yourself there in the land of the Perizzite and the Rephaim, since the hill country of Ephraim is too narrow for you.'\" This is the language of this baraita, and it appears from it that the land was divided according to their skull-count, according to the words of the Rav. But according to the Gemara it is a difficulty. Furthermore, if so, why would the children of Joseph cry out? After all, they were given according to their large number just like all the other tribes.\n\nIn my opinion there is an abbreviation in this baraita, and it is from what they said in the Gemara (Bava Batra 118a): because of the minors who became numerous, the children of Joseph cried out. And the explanation of the matter is: the children of Joseph took like two tribes — the children of Ephraim one portion and the children of Manasseh one portion, for so it is written (Joshua 14:4) — and not one of them is more numerous in population than the rest of the tribes; rather, among the other tribes there are some more numerous than they, for the tribe of Judah, Issachar, Zebulun, and Dan are more numerous than they. So why should they cry out, and the more numerous ones remain silent? All the more so if the land was divided among those who went out from Egypt according to its midrash, for in the first census the children of Judah were more numerous than these two tribes combined. Rather, they were crying out because of the minors. For the children of Manasseh in the census of the exodus from Egypt were thirty-two thousand, and they increased in the second census in the wilderness to fifty-two thousand, and there is no tribe among all the tribes that increased so much. And they continued to multiply sons in this manner until the land was divided, and likewise the children of Ephraim. And those minors would receive nothing at all; therefore they cried out, but they had no one to listen to them, for such is the law — that none but those twenty years old and above would receive. Therefore the baraita said \"according to his counted ones,\" meaning those who were twenty years old and above. This teaches that the land was not divided except to each and every tribe according to what it was at the time of the census, so that they would give nothing at all to the minors, even if they grew and reached twenty years of age at the time of the division.\n\nAnd the verse that says \"Why have you given me a possession of one lot and one portion, and I am a numerous people\" — according to everyone's words, it is impossible for it to remain in its plain sense on the surface level. For if he divided the land among them according to their skull-count, as per Rashi's words — behold, they took enough, and just as they are a numerous people they took a large portion. And if it was by tribes — it is impossible that Joshua did not give them the portion of their firstborn status, for he would not transgress the command of Jacob, and also the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded in the Torah regarding the tribes of Ephraim and Manasseh. And furthermore it is explicitly written that he gave them two lots, as it is written: \"And the border of the children of Ephraim was according to their families,\" \"This is the inheritance of the tribe of the children of Ephraim according to their families\" (Joshua 16:5, 8), and concerning the children of Manasseh: \"And the lot fell to the tribe of Manasseh,\" \"And the border of Manasseh was\" (Joshua 17:1, 7), like everything said concerning the other tribes. Rather, what they said was \"one lot and one portion\" to mean: all that you gave to the two of us is fitting for one alone in his lot. And they used this expression because Joshua first cast one lot for both of them, as it is said \"And the lot came out for the children of Joseph from the Jordan at Jericho,\" etc., until \"and its goings out were westward\" (Joshua 16:1–3), and there it says \"And the children of Joseph, Manasseh and Ephraim, inherited\" (v. 4), meaning that the two of them inherited in this lot. And afterward he divided that lot between the two of them by lot, as he said there \"And the border of the children of Ephraim was,\" etc., and it is written \"And the lot fell to the tribe of Manasseh,\" etc. Therefore they said: behold, the one first lot is fitting for each one of us two, for we are a numerous people, so why did you afterward divide it into two portions?\n\nAnd the complaint was because of the minors who became numerous among them, according to the way of the Gemara.\n\nAnd according to the way of the plain sense, it appears to me that this outcry was that of the children of Manasseh, only that the two tribes came before him together. And similarly, \"And the children of Judah drew near to Joshua at Gilgal, and Caleb son of Jephunneh said to him\" (Joshua 14:6) — the claim is for one individual, and the entire tribe pleads together with him. And the complaint of the children of Manasseh was because among all the tribes there is none that left many large cities to the Canaanite as Manasseh did — the three regions of the lowland (Joshua 17:11), three districts. And a proof for the matter: for after it is written \"And the children of Manasseh could not drive out the inhabitants of these cities\" (v. 12), there it is written \"And the children of Joseph spoke\" (v. 14), for at first they did not complain until they waged war against them and could not overcome them.\n\nAnd it is possible that this is the reason for \"one lot and one portion\" — they said: because you first cast one lot for the two of us, our portion fell together, and the territory for both of us chanced to be in this difficult place. But had you made for us two lots as is the custom for any two tribes, it would have come out in another place that is easy to conquer. And Joshua said to them that if they go up to the forest of the Perizzite and the Rephaim, they would clear for themselves there whatever they wish and expand their territory. And they returned and clarified their words and said: even the mountain will not suffice for us, for the Canaanites whom you have given us in the valley land all have iron chariots. And then Joshua said to them that he would not give them one lot on the mountain, but rather the entire mountain would be theirs, and the great forest that is there, and they would clear from it everything they need, and all the outskirts of the mountain's borders would be theirs, and they would drive out the Canaanite from there — for he has iron chariots and he is strong, and the other tribes do not desire him, but they are two brothers who are a numerous people and have great strength; they will help one another and drive him out.\n\nAnd in the end he did not listen to them and did not add anything to them, for such is the law.\n\nNow, the need has broadened our words on this matter, and we have also written them with proofs in the portion of Vayehi Yaakov. And the principle is that the law they raised in the Gemara is true, and by it the verses should be explained.\n\nAnd now, the explanation of the pericope: \"To these the land shall be divided as an inheritance by the number of names\" — to those mentioned by families, the land shall be divided by the count of names, that they shall give each male his portion according to their skull-count. \"To the large you shall increase his inheritance\" — that they shall divide the land of Reuben into four portions: to the family of the Hanochites, the large one, for example, we shall increase its inheritance; and to the family of the Palluite, the small one, we shall diminish. For each man according to the counted ones of his family shall his inheritance be given, and each entire family shall take in one place. And for this reason He counts them here by families. Therefore the Sages mentioned (Sifrei Zuta, BeHar, chapter 1, law 2): They divided by families and did not divide by fathers' houses, and no one individual recognized what was his own, etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ה׳ is resolved as HaShem throughout. The Sifrei Zuta citation follows the Hebrew abbreviation ת״כ בהר; I rendered the chapter-law reference as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:44.959613246Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}